---
import CaseStudyLayout from "../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro";
---

<CaseStudyLayout
	slug="tech4good-lab"
	title="Tech4Good Lab"
	subtitle="Lab Operations & Literature Review"
	tags={["Research Ops", "Leadership", "Literature Review"]}
	summary="Led research teams across AI, design, and web projects, building leadership and workflow systems that improved cross-lab communication and collaboration."
>
	<Fragment slot="problem">
		<p>
			As the lab grew to support concurrent AI, design, and web projects, coordination
			started to break down. Researchers on different project teams didn't have a
			consistent way to share findings, onboard new members, or track where a given
			project stood.
		</p>
		<p class="placeholder">
			[PLACEHOLDER: a specific breakdown or incident that motivated the systems work]
		</p>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="process">
		<ul>
			<li>
				Mapped the existing, informal workflows across project teams to identify where
				communication was breaking down — onboarding, weekly syncs, literature review
				handoffs, and deliverable tracking.
			</li>
			<li>
				Interviewed <span class="placeholder">[PLACEHOLDER: number]</span> lab members across
				roles — undergraduate researchers, project leads, and the faculty advisor — to
				understand what a good system would need to support.
			</li>
			<li>
				Designed and piloted a standardized onboarding process and documentation
				structure so new researchers could get oriented without requiring one-on-one
				ramp-up time from a lead every time.
			</li>
			<li>
				Built a lightweight cross-lab communication rhythm — shared updates and
				structured syncs — so leads across different projects had visibility into each
				other's progress and blockers.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="decisions">
		<ul>
			<li>
				Prioritized lightweight, low-maintenance systems — templates, shared docs, a
				simple update cadence — over heavier project management tooling, since adoption
				mattered more than sophistication for a volunteer research team.
			</li>
			<li>
				Made documentation a required deliverable of every literature review, not an
				afterthought, so institutional knowledge stayed with the lab rather than leaving
				when a researcher graduated.
			</li>
			<li>
				Delegated ownership of specific systems — onboarding, the literature review
				pipeline, syncs — to individual team leads rather than centralizing everything, to
				build leadership capacity across the lab instead of a single point of failure.
			</li>
		</ul>
	</Fragment>

	<Fragment slot="outcome">
		<p class="placeholder">
			[PLACEHOLDER: quantified result, e.g. "Cut new-researcher ramp-up time from X weeks
			to Y, across Z researchers and N concurrent projects."]
		</p>
		<p class="placeholder">
			[PLACEHOLDER: any lasting artifact, e.g. "Onboarding system adopted lab-wide and
			still in use as of [date]."]
		</p>
	</Fragment>
</CaseStudyLayout>
